Phase 2: CSS3 Practical

HTML provides the structure, but CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) provides the beauty. Without CSS, the web would be a very boring place consisting of plain text on white backgrounds.

In this phase, we will learn how to master layouts, colors, typography, and animations. 

---

1. CSS3 Basics & Selectors

CSS works by "selecting" HTML elements and applying "styles" to them.


/* Select by tag name */
h1 {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 32px;
}

/* Select by class name (use a dot) */
.highlight {
    background-color: yellow;
}

/* Select by ID (use a hash) */
#main-header {
    text-align: center;
}


---

2. The Box Model

The Box Model is the most important concept in CSS. Every HTML element is essentially a box wrapped in layers:

1. **Content:** The actual text or image.
2. **Padding:** Space inside the box, around the content.
3. **Border:** A line surrounding the padding.
4. **Margin:** Space outside the box, separating it from other elements.


.box {
    width: 200px;
    padding: 20px;
    border: 2px solid black;
    margin: 10px;
}


---

3. Flexbox Layout

Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout method for arranging items in rows or columns. It makes alignment incredibly easy.


.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center; /* Centers items horizontally */
    align-items: center;     /* Centers items vertically */
    gap: 15px;               /* Space between items */
}


---

4. Grid Layout

CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system (rows and columns) perfect for complex webpage layouts (like galleries or dashboards).


.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); /* 3 equal columns */
    gap: 20px;
}


---

5. Responsive Design

Websites must look good on phones, tablets, and desktops. We use Media Queries to change styles based on screen size.


/* Default for mobile */
.container {
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* For screens wider than 768px (Tablets/Desktops) */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
    .container {
        flex-direction: row;
    }
}


---

6. Animations & Transitions

Make your website feel alive!


.button {
    background-color: blue;
    transition: all 0.3s ease; /* Smooth transition */
}

.button:hover {
    background-color: darkblue;
    transform: scale(1.05); /* Slightly enlarge on hover */
}


---

🎓 Micro-Project: Style Your Profile Page

Remember the `profile.html` we built in Phase 1? It's time to make it look professional!

1. Create a `styles.css` file and link it in your HTML `<head>`.
2. Add a global font (e.g., sans-serif).
3. Use **Flexbox** to center your profile picture and name.
4. Give your image a circular shape using `border-radius: 50%`.
5. Style the form inputs with padding and borders.
6. Add a hover effect to your Submit button.

Phase 3: JavaScript Practical

HTML is the skeleton. CSS is the skin. JavaScript (JS) is the brain and muscle. It is the programming language of the web that enables interactive web pages.

---

1. JavaScript Basics

Like any programming language, JS has variables, data types, and logic.


// Variables
let age = 20; // Can be changed
const name = "Rahul"; // Cannot be changed

// Arrays and Objects
const techStack = ["HTML", "CSS", "JS"];
const user = {
    name: "Rahul",
    role: "Developer"
};

// Conditional Logic
if (age >= 18) {
    console.log("You can access this site.");
} else {
    console.log("Too young.");
}


---

2. DOM Manipulation

The Document Object Model (DOM) is a representation of your HTML that JavaScript can read and modify.


// Selecting an element
const heading = document.getElementById("main-heading");

// Changing text and styles
heading.innerText = "Welcome to JavaScript!";
heading.style.color = "red";


---

3. Events

JS can listen for user actions (clicks, typing, scrolling) and respond to them.


const button = document.querySelector("#submit-btn");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    alert("Button was clicked!");
});


---

4. Functions & ES6+ Features

Functions allow you to write reusable blocks of code. ES6 introduced modern, cleaner syntax.


// Traditional Function
function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

// ES6 Arrow Function (Modern)
const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;

// Template Literals (String formatting)
console.log(`Hello, my name is ${user.name}`);


---

5. Local Storage

Save data directly in the user's browser so it persists even if they refresh the page.


// Save data
localStorage.setItem("theme", "dark");

// Retrieve data
const currentTheme = localStorage.getItem("theme");
console.log(currentTheme); // "dark"


---

6. APIs & Fetch

The real power of JS comes from fetching data from external servers (APIs) asynchronously.


// Fetching random user data
fetch("https://randomuser.me/api/")
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log(data.results[0].name.first);
    })
    .catch(error => console.error("Error fetching data:", error));


---

🎓 Micro-Project: Build a To-Do List

Let's build a classic project: a To-Do App!

1. Create a simple UI with an `<input>` field, an "Add" `<button>`, and an empty `<ul>`.
2. Write JS to capture the input text when the button is clicked.
3. Create a new `<li>` element using `document.createElement()`, add the text to it, and append it to the `<ul>`.
4. (Bonus) Add a "Delete" button next to each item to remove it from the DOM.
5. (Super Bonus) Save the list to `localStorage`!

Phase 4: React Practical

React is a JavaScript library created by Facebook for building user interfaces. Instead of modifying the DOM manually (which is slow and messy for large apps), React lets you build reusable "Components".

---

1. React Basics & Components

A React application is just a tree of components. Components are basically JavaScript functions that return HTML (this syntax is called JSX).


// A simple React Component
function Welcome() {
    return (
        <div className="card">
            <h1>Welcome to React!</h1>
            <p>This is my first component.</p>
        </div>
    );
}

export default Welcome;


---

2. Props & State

- **Props:** How we pass data *down* to components (read-only).
- **State:** How a component manages its *own* changing data over time.


import { useState } from 'react';

function Counter({ title }) {
    // State: [currentValue, functionToUpdateValue]
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{title}</h2> {/* Using Props */}
            <p>Current Count: {count}</p>
            <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
                Increment
            </button>
        </div>
    );
}


---

3. Essential Hooks (useEffect)

`useEffect` allows you to perform side effects in your components, such as fetching data from an API when the component first loads.


import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function UserList() {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);

    useEffect(() => {
        // This runs once when the component mounts
        fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users")
            .then(res => res.json())
            .then(data => setUsers(data));
    }, []); // Empty array means run only once

    return (
        <ul>
            {users.map(user => (
                <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>
            ))}
        </ul>
    );
}


---

4. React Router

React builds Single Page Applications (SPAs). React Router lets you navigate between different "pages" without the browser ever reloading.


import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';

function App() {
    return (
        <BrowserRouter>
            <nav>
                <Link to="/">Home</Link>
                <Link to="/about">About</Link>
            </nav>

            <Routes>
                <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
                <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
            </Routes>
        </BrowserRouter>
    );
}


---

🎓 Micro-Project: Movie Search App

Build an application that searches for movies using the OMDB API!

1. Create a `MovieCard` component that accepts movie details as Props (Title, Poster, Year).
2. Create a main `App` component with state for `searchTerm` and `movies` array.
3. Add a search input and a button.
4. When the button is clicked, use `fetch` inside React to call the OMDB API with the search term.
5. Update the `movies` state with the results, and `map()` over them to render a list of `MovieCard` components!

Phase 5: Node.js & MongoDB

Frontend development is fun, but to build real-world applications (like E-Commerce, Social Media, or Dashboards), you need a backend to process logic, authenticate users, and a database to store data permanently.

---

1. Node.js & Express.js Basics

Node.js allows you to run JavaScript on the server. Express.js is a framework that makes building Node.js servers incredibly simple.


// server.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = 5000;

// Allow JSON parsing
app.use(express.json());

// A simple GET route
app.get('/api/hello', (req, res) => {
    res.json({ message: "Hello from the backend!" });
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});


---

2. RESTful APIs

APIs are the bridge between your React frontend and your Node.js backend. We use standard HTTP methods for CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations.

- `GET`: Fetch data.
- `POST`: Create new data.
- `PUT`: Update existing data.
- `DELETE`: Remove data.


// POST route to add a new user
app.post('/api/users', (req, res) => {
    const newUser = req.body;
    // Save to database logic here...
    res.status(201).json({ message: "User created", data: newUser });
});


---

3. MongoDB & Mongoose

MongoDB is a NoSQL database that stores data in JSON-like documents (perfect for JavaScript developers). Mongoose is an elegant tool to interact with MongoDB.


const mongoose = require('mongoose');

// Connect to Database
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost:27017/myDatabase')
    .then(() => console.log("Connected to MongoDB!"))
    .catch(err => console.error(err));

// Define a Schema and Model
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: { type: String, required: true },
    email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true },
    password: { type: String, required: true }
});

const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);


---

4. Authentication (JWT)

Security is crucial. JSON Web Tokens (JWT) allow us to verify if a user is logged in before letting them access protected routes.

1. User sends email/password to login route.
2. Server verifies and creates a signed token string.
3. Server sends token to frontend.
4. Frontend sends token in the header for every subsequent request.

---

🎓 Micro-Project: Build a REST API

1. Initialize a new Node.js project (`npm init -y`) and install `express` and `mongoose`.
2. Create a server that connects to a local MongoDB database.
3. Create a `Note` model (Title, Content, Date).
4. Build the full CRUD API:
   - `GET /api/notes` (Get all notes)
   - `POST /api/notes` (Create a note)
   - `PUT /api/notes/:id` (Update a specific note)
   - `DELETE /api/notes/:id` (Delete a specific note)
5. Test your API using Postman or ThunderClient!
